        "     output is painted as a few frames rather than one frame per line. When you scroll up, the emulator simply",
        "     changes the offset and repaints the newly exposed region, which is why scrolling through thousands of lines",
        "     feels instant. Many modern terminals cap the number of preserved lines, while dedicated applications such as",
        "     iTerm2 keep very large buffers at the cost of memory. In principle, scrollback is thus an illusion sustained by",
        "     a growing in-memory history that the emulator re-renders on demand, distinct from the physical continuous paper",
